WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
The last medical emergency we'll talk about is fainting.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.880
Fainting happens when blood flow to the brain suddenly drops,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.720
often triggered by things like standing too long, dehydration,

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.640
or even just getting overheated.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.719
If you see someone faint, your first step is to make sure the area is safe

00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:18.719
and help them lie down flat on their back

00:00:18.719 --> 00:00:21.120
so you can check their breathing and responsiveness.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.480
If they seem too hot, take a moment to help cool them down.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.920
It is important to remember that you should avoid moving the person,

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.639
especially if you suspect a head or neck injury,

00:00:30.639 --> 00:00:32.720
unless the scene itself becomes dangerous

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.919
or you have to move them to perform life-saving CPR.

00:00:35.919 --> 00:00:39.599
You'll want to call 911 if they stay unresponsive for more than a minute

00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:43.119
or don't improve quickly, or if they were injured when they fell.

00:00:43.119 --> 00:00:47.919
Always call for professional help if an infant, young child, or pregnant person faints.

00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:50.000
To prevent a faint before it happens,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.959
if a person tells you they feel dizzy or lightheaded,

00:00:52.959 --> 00:00:55.520
have them sit or lie down immediately.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.799
Crossing their legs or leaning over with their head between their knees

00:00:58.799 --> 00:01:01.119
can often help that dizzy feeling pass.

